      <workList xml:id="workDesc_N20455">
         <work xml:id="work_ida620f38d">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_N20459">C3:002</identifier>
            <title xml:id="title_id9bfb3143" xml:lang="de">Misogynis wohlgegründete Ursachen das weibliche Geschlecht zu verachten</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_N20467"/>
            <creation xml:id="creation_idaac01237">
               <date isodate="1750" xml:id="date_N20470">1750</date>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_idf2ac9199">
               <p xml:id="idm175">One of the first books that Scheibe wrote while living in Sønderborg was his satirical essay, <rend xml:id="idm176" fontstyle="italic">Misogynis</rend> (that is, the women hater), which received at least three editions during the next fourteen years: first in 1750, then sometime between 1750 and 1764, and finally in 1764. However, no copy of the second edition seems to have survived. There is no indication as to the reason why Scheibe published his <rend xml:id="idm177" fontstyle="italic">Misogynis</rend> though he does seem to have have had a grudge against Danish women. In his <rend xml:id="idm178" fontstyle="italic">Anekdoten</rend> (1771; SchW C3:001), they are characterised as being quite dominant and ruling in the homes, with boots and spurs riding the wildest stallions, and will soon be wearing men’s clothes; men, on the other hand, have become effeminate and soft. Women of the lowest class are so sensitive that they continuously freeze, both winter and summer, and therefore constantly wear large furs with lining. In the third edition of <rend xml:id="idm179" fontstyle="italic">Misogynis</rend>, Scheibe has added a note to his introduction explaining that 'even if the entire content of this little book of nothing but iniquities deals with females, one cannot but also think of the malice of a certain despicable man who, from a mere interested intention of a bread-and-butter thievish kind, reprinted the rightful publisher, and thus made himself a life-long stinking name' (sig. )(3v). The reference might be to the second volume, <rend xml:id="idm180" fontstyle="italic">Die allerneueste erfundene Art</rend> (1753; SchW C7:001), and hence Scheibe was not the author of that work. The name of the author does not appear on the title page but according to Holzmann &amp; Bohatta, <rend xml:id="idm181" fontstyle="italic">Deutsches Anonymenlexikon</rend>, the pseud. for Misogynis is Scheibe.</p>
            </history>
